 {"id":"CF-147","title":"P3 — the CF-123 census-port fix is protected by no gate arm: reverting one token of the call site keeps the single-run gate green while two concurrent runs red each other again","status":"pending","priority":"P3","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-04T02:38:28Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T02:38:28Z","detail":{"fingerprint":"495c612cd16435d71bbc00df25e8fd8ae9501e556df690ab7cf33259b76d73ff"},"reasoning":"Review of commit 72d51bc (\"fix(selftest): census fixtures bind ephemeral ports so concurrent gate runs cannot red each other. Addresses CF-117.\"), measured 2026-11-03 21:24-21:26 -05 by tick code-factory-review-2026-11-04-02-20-15 from throwaway clones (family barrier live: sibling lanes writing the board).\n\nWHAT THE COMMIT GETS RIGHT (measured, not read):\n1. The new suite is load-bearing. Overlaying the tip's tests/selftest.sh on the true parent 25d1e71 (whose cf089-census-standin.py has no --port-file) reds all eight census arms with NAMED rows and rc=1: \"census fetch guard (exit 99): fixture did not start on port unknown: usage: cf089-census-standin.py [-h] [--port PORT]\" and the seven siblings -> SELFTEST: FAIL (8). The same parent tree with its OWN pre-fix suite is SELFTEST: PASS rc=0, so the overlay, not the tree, is what reds.\n2. The port class is gone. Six whole-gate runs at the tip in three rounds of two concurrent clones produced port_red_lines=0 (no bind failure, no \"Failed to connect to 127.0.0.1 port 190xx\"), and the single arm produced none either.\n3. The doc is honest about its residual: docs/audits/code-factory-perf-2026-11-03-concurrency.md states that two concurrent runs still red each other through scripts/verify-node.sh:42's fixed /tmp/node-status.json (CF-072 AC-2), and its own table shows 2 of 3 rounds with a red arm.\n\nTHE DEFECT (this row):\nThe property the commit establishes is protected by nothing in the gate. cf089_run passes \"--port 0 --port-file $tmpd/port\" on one line of tests/selftest.sh; replacing that \"--port 0\" with a literal port (the pre-fix shape, one token) leaves the whole gate green:\n\n  sed -i 's/--port 0 --port-file/--port 19022 --port-file/' tests/selftest.sh\n  bash tests/selftest.sh                      -> SELFTEST: PASS rc=0        (mutation invisible)\n  two concurrent runs of the same mutation    -> both arms rc=1, FAIL (5) / FAIL (4), 10 and 8 port-red lines\n\nSo the regression that CF-117 cost a P2 row and a measured control to fix can come back through a single-token edit, and the only instrument that would catch it is a manual two-arm control that no tick re-runs. Old CF-117's core sentence — \"two gate runs at one HEAD red each other\" — becomes true again the moment someone \"simplifies\" the call site, with the gate still green.\n\nRelated, recorded but NOT filed as a row (documentation/provenance, no executable defect):\n- The commit SUBJECT (\"cannot red each other\") is stronger than its evidence: 1 of 6 concurrent arms red in this tick's sample and the commit's own audit doc prints 2 of 3 rounds with a red arm. The doc attributes those reds to CF-072 and states the residual in prose, so the code is right and the durable explanation exists; only the git subject overstates, and a commit body is immutable, so tightening the message is not available after the fact.\n- The commit's ch:trace marker reads row=CF-117. Today CF-117 is an unrelated pending finding (the publish-drift census), and the row that carries 72d51bc is CF-123 — the ports row was filed under the already-taken id CF-117 at 15:47 (2ab0f85), closed at 15:49 (31da857), then renumbered on merge (e42f267). CF-123's own reasoning already records that renumber, so the ledger does carry the binding; what is missing is only a forward pointer from CF-117, which is why this is recorded here rather than filed as a separate row.\n\nAcceptance criterion 1 asks the gate to own the property; it does not ask for a new product feature, and no assertion in the existing suite is loosened — the 53 named PASS rows stay byte-identical (verified against 25d1e71) with the new arm's rows added on top.","acceptance_criteria":"AC0 — reproduce on the current tree from the repo root. The probe prints `mutated-single rc=0` and `conc-a rc=1` / `conc-b rc=1` today; at the fixed tip the same probe must print non-zero for `mutated-single` (a named FAIL row) with the two concurrent arms unchanged in meaning:\n\n    tmp=$(mktemp -d)\n    git clone -q . \"$tmp/a\"\n    git clone -q . \"$tmp/b\"\n    sed -i 's/--port 0 --port-file/--port 19022 --port-file/' \"$tmp/a/tests/selftest.sh\" \"$tmp/b/tests/selftest.sh\"\n    ( cd \"$tmp/a\" && bash tests/selftest.sh >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"mutated-single rc=$?\" )\n    ( cd \"$tmp/a\" && bash tests/selftest.sh >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"conc-a rc=$?\" ) &\n    ( cd \"$tmp/b\" && bash tests/selftest.sh >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"conc-b rc=$?\" ) &\n    wait\n    rm -rf \"$tmp\"\n\nMeasured 2026-11-03 21:37 -05 at 1da7f24: mutated-single rc=0, conc-a rc=1, conc-b rc=1 (identical at 47a0490 at 21:26; `git diff --name-only 47a0490 1da7f24 -- tests/ scripts/ specs/` = 0, so the pre-fix measurement carries).\n\nAC1 — the gate owns the census-port property. tests/selftest.sh gains an arm that starts TWO census fixtures concurrently (two cf089_run invocations, or two stand-in processes) and requires that each published a DISTINCT port and that both readiness probes answered 200 on their own port; the arm prints one named PASS row when they are distinct and one named FAIL row naming both ports when they collide. Deleting or weakening the arm turns the gate red only through that named row.\n\nAC2 — the arm is load-bearing against the exact regression. With AC1 landed, AC0's mutation of the call site makes the suite exit non-zero with that named FAIL row, and the same mutation on the pre-AC1 tree still prints SELFTEST: PASS (the control that proves the arm, not the sed, is what reds).\n\nAC3 — nothing is loosened. `bash -n tests/selftest.sh` and `python3 -m py_compile docs/audits/cf089-census-standin.py` exit 0; the sorted set of existing PASS row labels at the new tree is a superset of the 53 labels at 25d1e71 with no rename, no deletion and no change of assertion strength; `bash tests/selftest.sh` is SELFTEST: PASS rc=0 on a quiet host and the 8 census arms (§22-§27) stay green.\n\nAC4 — the residual stays attributed, not hidden. The new arm does not touch scripts/verify-node.sh; the CF-072 auth-arm race must still be reproducible at the new tree (two concurrent runs, one arm red on an auth row) and must NOT be masked by a lock or by serialising the suite.","probe":"tmp=$(mktemp -d)\ngit clone -q . \"$tmp/a\"\ngit clone -q . \"$tmp/b\"\nsed -i 's/--port 0 --port-file/--port 19022 --port-file/' \"$tmp/a/tests/selftest.sh\" \"$tmp/b/tests/selftest.sh\"\n( cd \"$tmp/a\" && bash tests/selftest.sh >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"mutated-single rc=$?\" )\n( cd \"$tmp/a\" && bash tests/selftest.sh >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"conc-a rc=$?\" ) &\n( cd \"$tmp/b\" && bash tests/selftest.sh >/dev/null 2>&1; echo \"conc-b rc=$?\" ) &\nwait\nrm -rf \"$tmp\""}
 {"id":"CF-148","title":"P2 — CF-053 was closed against an isolation criterion the fleet violates, and lane_target.py scan certifies a shared checkout as ok","status":"pending","priority":"P2","complexity":3,"created_at":"2026-11-04T02:39:07Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T02:39:07Z","detail":{"evidence":[{"run_id":"code-factory-duckbrain-qa-2026-11-04-02-20-15","ts":"2026-11-04T02:37:00Z","artifacts":["docs/audits/code-factory-qa-duckbrain-2026-11-04.md"]}]},"reasoning":"CF-053's acceptance criterion 4 reads: 'Concurrent lanes do not share one mutable checkout: two lanes must not write the same board from the same working tree.' Its closure evidence is the opposite of that criterion — the 18 satellite lanes were repointed ONTO one checkout. Measured 2026-11-04 by the QA lane: 'select count(*), count(distinct workdir) from projects where name like code-factory% and enabled=1' returns 19 lanes / 1 workdir, and the family block in the fleet config names the same workdir for every lane, so both surfaces agree. The consequence is already on the record from a sibling lane: docs/audits/code-factory-pm-2026-11-04-02-20-15.md records a sibling commit landing on main in that shared checkout while its judge ran, with a second gitreins process live; git log since 2026-11-03 18:00 shows pm, sync, duckbrain, dogfood and qa interleaving commits into the one tree within minutes, and three sibling lanes were running on it simultaneously at this tick's start. The instrument that closed the row cannot see it: two lanes pointed at the same checkout scan bad: 0 / ok: true / rc=0 (probe below), tests/selftest.sh section 11 asserts target parsing and home-repo rejection only, and scripts/lane_target.py carries no duplicate-workdir leg. Distinct from CF-097 (the repo-url guard lost the workdir arm's not_home_repo check) and from CF-061 (verify passes on a mid-merge checkout): this is the missing SHARING leg, and it is the leg CF-053 AC4 promised.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree: the probe below prints \"bad\": 0, \"ok\": true and scan_rc=0 for two lanes whose workdir is the same checkout.\n1. scripts/lane_target.py scan reports a named failure when two or more rows resolve to one workdir: after the fix, the SAME probe exits non-zero and names both lane names plus the shared path, and a scan of the live code-factory family (19 lanes, 1 workdir) reds naming that path — the instrument must be able to fail on the fleet it certifies.\n2. tests/selftest.sh gains an arm in the lane-target section that exercises the new leg both ways: two rows on one workdir print a named FAIL, two distinct workdirs print PASS; bash tests/selftest.sh prints SELFTEST: PASS at the fixed tree.\n3. CF-053 criterion 4 is dispositioned on the board: either the family is split onto distinct targets and the live scan is green, or the criterion is replaced with a recorded decision naming the accepted risk. No complete row may assert a criterion the live fleet violates.\n4. Do NOT repoint the live lanes as a side effect of this fix: moving 19 production lanes is an owner decision. Land the gate and the selftest arm, keep CF-053 open for the decision, and record which of the two remedies the owner chose.","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); printf '{\"name\":\"lane-a\",\"workdir\":\"%s\",\"repo_url\":\"local:%s\",\"enabled\":true}\\n{\"name\":\"lane-b\",\"workdir\":\"%s\",\"repo_url\":\"local:%s\",\"enabled\":true}\\n' \"$PWD\" \"$PWD\" \"$PWD\" \"$PWD\" > \"$d/lanes.jsonl\"; python3 scripts/lane_target.py scan \"$d/lanes.jsonl\" | grep -E '\"(bad|ok)\"' | tail -2; echo \"scan_rc=${PIPESTATUS[0]}\"; rm -rf \"$d\""}
 {"id":"CF-149","title":"P4 — CF-123's concurrency probe counts a negative-fixture row as a cross-arm red: unique_red_rows=1 against its own documented 0","status":"pending","priority":"P4","complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-11-04T02:39:07Z","updated_at":"2026-11-04T02:39:07Z","detail":{"evidence":[{"run_id":"code-factory-duckbrain-qa-2026-11-04-02-20-15","ts":"2026-11-04T02:37:00Z","artifacts":["docs/audits/code-factory-qa-duckbrain-2026-11-04.md"]}]},"reasoning":"CF-123 closed the two-simultaneous-gate-runs defect and recorded a probe whose header documents the post-fix expectation 'a_rc=0, b_rc=0, both SELFTEST: PASS, unique_red_rows=0 in both arms'. Re-run by the QA lane at tip bafdb41 the operative claims hold (a_rc=0, b_rc=0, both SELFTEST: PASS, port_red_lines=0) but unique_red_rows=1 in EACH arm, so the probe contradicts its own documented output. The unique row in each arm is the same message — 'disk_floor — resolver missing or unreadable: <mktemp dir>/scripts/disk_floor.py' — a deliberate negative fixture asserted by tests/selftest.sh:509 whose text embeds $HERE, the arm's own mktemp path. The probe diffs raw FAIL-row text with comm, so a per-run path alone makes a row 'unique', and the counter cannot distinguish a real cross-arm race (the defect the probe exists to catch) from a negative arm that always prints. This is an instrument defect, not a regression of the port fix: the port fix reproduces.","acceptance_criteria":"0. Reproduce on the current tree: the probe below prints two 'unique_red_rows=1' lines, each followed by the same disk_floor negative-fixture message at a different temp path.\n1. The probe normalizes per-run paths (mktemp/scratch roots, ports) before the set difference, or restricts the comparison to rows that cannot carry a per-run path; at the fixed probe two concurrent PASS runs print unique_red_rows=0 in both arms while a_rc, b_rc, both SELFTEST lines and port_red_lines are unchanged.\n2. The red arm survives normalization: with one arm's tree mutated to emit a genuine extra failing row, the normalized counter reports it as unique for that arm only.\n3. The probe's own documented expectation and its measured output agree at the fixed tree, and CF-123's recorded probe field carries the corrected text so the next QA tick re-runs the version that matches its documentation.","probe":"d=$(mktemp -d); jq -r 'select(.id==\"CF-123\").probe' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl > \"$d/p.sh\"; bash \"$d/p.sh\" > \"$d/out\" 2>&1; grep -E 'unique_red_rows=|^    ' \"$d/out\"; rm -rf \"$d\""}
-{"id": "CF-150", "title": "P3 — the documented node create path can never start its gateway: no step in node-create.sh, project-node.sh or install-hermes.sh stages dashboard.basic_auth, and no doc says how", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T02:43:03Z", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T16:50:10Z", "detail": null, "reasoning": "Dogfood walk 2026-11-04 (tick code-factory-duckbrain-dogfood-2026-11-04-02-20-15) from a clean copy of origin/main @ 1da7f24, control host only. scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:94 greps ~/.hermes/config.yaml for basic_auth and :100 refuses gateway_start when it is absent; nothing in the create path writes that file (grep -c basic_auth and grep -c hermes/config.yaml are 0 for scripts/node-create.sh and scripts/project-node.sh), and node-create.sh --staged-files stages only project-node.sh, lib.sh, disk_floor.py, reclaim_disk.py and harness/install-<harness>.sh. Measured, verbatim transcript in docs/audits/cf-dogfood-2026-11-04-gateway-apply.txt: fresh HOME, env -i, HERMES_PYTHON_BIN=/usr/bin/python3.12, `bash scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh --apply` prints `PASS  install — Hermes Agent v0.19.0 (2026.7.20)` followed by `FAIL  gateway_start — refused: a public bind needs dashboard.basic_auth in ~/.hermes/config.yaml` and `HERMES: FAIL (4)` rc=1. docs/OPERATIONS.md:80 states the requirement but names no command that stages the block; the README's step 3 ('make one') does not mention it. Consequence: a reader following README step 3 ends NODE INCOMPLETE, verify-node.sh gateway_reachable cannot pass, and the Status table's round trip (CF-004) is unreachable by the documented path. Distinct from CF-071 (that row is about the gate's substring validation being too LENIENT once a config exists; this row is that no documented step produces the config at all). Raised from P3 to P1 on 2026-11-05 after reading the branch structure: the auth gate at scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:202-210 is reached only from the NOT-LISTENING branch (:240 `if [ \"$MODE\" = \"--apply\" ]`), while the listening branch (:232-238) checks only the port and /api/status. A gateway already bound to 0.0.0.0 with no dashboard.basic_auth is therefore certified `PASS port` + `PASS status_probe` — an authenticated probe from 127.0.0.1 does not prove the public bind is protected.", "acceptance_criteria": "0. Reproduce on the current tree (from the repo root): `jq -r 'select(.id==\"CF-150\").probe' .coding-hermes/board/tasks.jsonl > p.sh && bash p.sh` prints `installer_basic_auth=3 create_path_basic_auth=scripts/node-create.sh:0,scripts/project-node.sh:0, create_path_config_yaml=scripts/node-create.sh:0,scripts/project-node.sh:0,`, prints the `scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh:100` refusal line, and exits 0 today.\n1. One documented, scripted step stages a node `~/.hermes/config.yaml` carrying a non-empty `dashboard.basic_auth` block, with the credential supplied as a mode-600 file (never an argument, per AGENTS.md rule 8), so `install-hermes.sh --apply` in a fresh HOME reaches the launch path instead of the refusal: the run prints a launch or reachability row for `gateway_start` rather than `refused: a public bind needs dashboard.basic_auth`.\n2. Negative control in the same run: with the staged block removed, `--apply` still prints the exact refusal wording and exits nonzero, so the new step is a producer and not a weakened gate.\n3. `README.md` (step 3 or the Status table) and `docs/OPERATIONS.md` name who stages the block and with which command, and state what a freshly created node is expected to reach; the text matches the command the tree ships.\n4. `bash tests/selftest.sh` prints `SELFTEST: PASS` at the fixed tip, with a numbered section that fails on the pre-fix tree (fixture: staged config present -> gate passes; absent -> named refusal), and `scripts/boardctl validate` prints `RESULT: OK`.", "probe": "echo \"installer_basic_auth=$(grep -c basic_auth scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh) create_path_basic_auth=$(grep -c basic_auth scripts/node-create.sh scripts/project-node.sh | tr '\\n' ',') create_path_config_yaml=$(grep -c hermes/config.yaml scripts/node-create.sh scripts/project-node.sh | tr '\\n' ',') \"; grep -n 'refused: a public bind needs dashboard.basic_auth' scripts/harness/install-hermes.sh; echo rc=0"}
